<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-3">
  <title>Quiz: One-Proportion Confidence Intervals Attempt 3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>Confidence interval formulas and R command</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\dfrac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>SE=\sqrt{\dfrac{\widehat{p}(1-\widehat{p})}{n}}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\alpha=1-C</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z^*=\texttt{qnorm(1-alpha/2)}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>E=z^*\cdot SE</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\text{CI}=(\widehat{p}-E,\widehat{p}+E)</m>
      </p>
      <p>Here <m>C</m> is the confidence level written as a decimal.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Use the one-proportion normal-approximation method and assume its
      conditions are satisfied. Show your work or R commands. Keep full
      precision in intermediate calculations and report interval endpoints
      as proportions rounded to four decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- Source: 1propCI.docx, Question 9; changed the one-sided upper
       confidence bound to a two-sided 90% confidence interval. -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-3-ticks" workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A regional surveillance program has a collection of 22,000 ticks.
        Researchers select a simple random sample of 220 ticks without
        replacement for laboratory testing, and 33 carry a particular
        pathogen. Calculate a two-sided 90% confidence interval for the
        proportion of ticks in the collection that carry this pathogen.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Source: 1propCI.docx, Question 7; added a comparison of interval
       widths while retaining the original data and confidence levels. -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-3-seeds" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A botanist selects a simple random sample of 380 seeds without
        replacement from a lot containing 38,000 seeds. Under a standardized
        germination protocol, 323 of the sampled seeds germinate. Use this
        sample to calculate both a 92% and a 95% confidence interval for the
        proportion of all seeds in the lot that would germinate under this
        protocol. Which interval is wider, and why does increasing the
        confidence level change the width when the sample is unchanged?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
